    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The Farm’s Spartacus is a defining document of the early-1990s “baggy” crossover, blending rock guitars, dance-floor rhythms, synthesizers and psychedelic groove. Released in 1991, the album captures the Liverpool band at the point where indie rock and rave culture were converging, balancing the driving pulse of “Groovy Train” with the anthemic optimism of “All Together Now.” Other highlights, including “Hearts & Minds,” “How Long” and “Don’t Let Me Down,” give the record a lively, communal energy, even as its loose, unpolished character reflects the era’s preference for movement and atmosphere over studio perfection. Spartacus reached number one in the UK and received generally positive contemporary attention, establishing The Farm as prominent figures in the period’s dance-rock landscape.\n\nDemon Records’ reissue presents the original album on Spartacus red, white and green tri-colour vinyl—the first colour-vinyl edition and the first reissue since its original 1991 release. The edition also includes brand-new sleeve notes by author Kevin Sampson, who was The Farm’s former manager, adding historical context to an album closely associated with the band’s breakthrough and the wider cultural momentum of 1991.",
